                <h1 class="content-title">Master Data Model & <span class="gradient-text">CSDM 4.0</span></h1>
                <p class="content-subtitle">
                    Master the foundational relational schema of ServiceNow ITSM — the polymorphic `task` table 
                    inheritance model, database flattening, many-to-many intersections, and CSDM 4.0 service mapping.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 1: The task Hierarchy -->
            <section class="content-section" id="task-inheritance">
                <h2>The <code>task</code> Super-Table Hierarchy</h2>
                <p>
                    The architectural cornerstone of ServiceNow ITSM is the <strong><code>task</code></strong> table. 
                    Nearly every operational ITSM record — including Incidents, Problems, Change Requests, and Service Requests — 
                    is an extended child table inheriting structure and business logic from <code>task</code>.
                </p>

                <div class="diagram-block">
                    <div class="diagram-title">ITSM Task Table Inheritance Tree</div>
                    <div class="mermaid">
                    classDiagram
                        class task {
                            +GUID sys_id
                            +String number
                            +String short_description
                            +String description
                            +Integer state
                            +Integer priority
                            +Integer urgency
                            +Integer impact
                            +Reference assignment_group
                            +Reference assigned_to
                            +Reference cmdb_ci
                            +DateTime opened_at
                            +DateTime closed_at
                        }

                        class incident {
                            +Reference caller_id
                            +Reference problem_id
                            +Reference rfc
                            +Reference parent_incident
                            +Choice hold_reason
                            +Choice close_code
                            +String close_notes
                            +Choice major_incident_state
                        }

                        class problem {
                            +Reference first_reported_by_task
                            +String workaround
                            +String cause_notes
                            +Choice resolution_code
                            +Boolean known_error
                        }

                        class change_request {
                            +Choice type
                            +Reference chg_model
                            +Choice risk
                            +Choice scope
                            +DateTime start_date
                            +DateTime end_date
                            +Choice review_status
                            +Boolean cab_required
                        }

                        class sc_request {
                            +Reference requested_for
                            +Currency price
                            +Choice stage
                            +Choice approval
                        }

                        class sc_req_item {
                            +Reference request
                            +Reference cat_item
                            +Currency price
                            +Integer quantity
                            +Choice stage
                        }

                        class sc_task {
                            +Reference request_item
                            +Reference request
                            +Choice state
                        }

                        task <|-- incident
                        task <|-- problem
                        task <|-- change_request
                        task <|-- sc_request
                        task <|-- sc_req_item
                        task <|-- sc_task
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                    <div class="info-card-title">Database Storage & Task Splitting</div>
                    <p>
                        In the underlying relational database (MariaDB / MySQL), ServiceNow uses <strong>Table Flattening</strong> 
                        (storing base fields in a consolidated physical table) combined with <strong>Task Splitting</strong> for ultra-high-volume tables. 
                        This architectural design ensures blazing fast polymorphic queries (e.g., <em>"Show all open tasks assigned to Network Group"</em> across incidents, problems, and changes with a single SQL query).
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 2: Polymorphic Fields & Dictionary Overrides -->
            <section class="content-section" id="polymorphic-fields">
                <h2>Inherited Fields & Dictionary Overrides</h2>
                <p>
                    Because child tables inherit all dictionary columns from <code>task</code>, ServiceNow utilizes 
                    <strong>Dictionary Overrides (<code>sys_dictionary_override</code>)</strong> to customize field behavior 
                    per child table without affecting the base <code>task</code> table or sibling modules.
                </p>

                <table class="data-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Field Name</th>
                            <th>Base <code>task</code> Definition</th>
                            <th><code>incident</code> Override</th>
                            <th><code>change_request</code> Override</th>
                            <th><code>sc_req_item</code> Override</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>state</code></td>
                            <td>Choice (Default: 1 - Open)</td>
                            <td>Values: 1(New), 2(In Progress), 3(On Hold), 6(Resolved), 7(Closed), 8(Canceled)</td>
                            <td>Values: -5(New), -4(Assess), -3(Authorize), -2(Scheduled), -1(Implement), 0(Review), 3(Closed), 4(Canceled)</td>
                            <td>Stage-driven states (1-Pending, 2-Open, 3-Closed Complete, 4-Closed Incomplete)</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>assigned_to</code></td>
                            <td>Optional Reference to <code>sys_user</code></td>
                            <td>Reference qual: dependent on <code>assignment_group</code></td>
                            <td>Mandatory in 'Implement' state</td>
                            <td>Assigned to technician fulfilling catalog task</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>close_notes</code></td>
                            <td>Optional String field</td>
                            <td>Mandatory upon moving to 'Resolved'</td>
                            <td>Mandatory during Post-Implementation Review (PIR)</td>
                            <td>Mandatory if Closed Incomplete</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>priority</code></td>
                            <td>Read-write Integer</td>
                            <td>Read-only (calculated by Data Lookup: Impact x Urgency)</td>
                            <td>Calculated via Risk Assessment & Priority matrix</td>
                            <td>Inherited from catalog item default</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 3: ERD Architecture -->
            <section class="content-section" id="erd-architecture">
                <h2>Entity Relationship Diagrams (ERD)</h2>
                <p>
                    Understanding how core operational tables interact with CMDB CIs, Knowledge Articles, and SLAs 
                    is essential for enterprise configuration and reporting:
                </p>

                <div class="diagram-block">
                    <div class="diagram-title">ITSM Core Entity Relationships</div>
                    <div class="mermaid">
                    erDiagram
                        task ||--o{ task_sla : "tracked by"
                        contract_sla ||--o{ task_sla : "defines rules for"
                        
                        incident ||--o{ incident_task : "delegates work to"
                        incident }o--o| problem : "investigated by"
                        problem ||--o{ problem_task : "decomposes into"
                        problem }o--o| change_request : "remediated by"
                        
                        incident }o--o| change_request : "caused by / resolves"
                        change_request ||--o{ change_task : "executes through"
                        
                        sc_request ||--|{ sc_req_item : "contains ordered items"
                        sc_req_item ||--|{ sc_task : "triggers fulfillment"
                        
                        task }o--o{ task_ci : "associates affected CIs"
                        task_ci }o--|| cmdb_ci : "points to"
                        
                        task }o--o{ task_cmdb_ci_service : "associates impacted services"
                        task_cmdb_ci_service }o--|| service_offering : "points to"
                        
                        problem ||--o{ kb_knowledge : "publishes Known Error"
                        incident ||--o{ kb_knowledge : "creates KCS Article"
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 4: CSDM 4.0 in ITSM -->
            <section class="content-section" id="csdm-alignment">
                <h2>CSDM 4.0 in ITSM Operations</h2>
                <p>
                    The <strong>Common Service Data Model (CSDM 4.0)</strong> provides a prescriptive framework for 
                    connecting ITSM processes directly to business value and infrastructure topology across 4 core domains:
                </p>

                <div class="diagram-block">
                    <div class="diagram-title">CSDM 4.0 Mapping to ITSM Tickets</div>
                    <div class="mermaid">
                    graph LR
                        subgraph SellConsume["1. Sell / Consume Domain"]
                            BS["Business Service<br>(cmdb_ci_service_business)"]
                            BSO["Business Service Offering<br>(service_offering)"]
                        end

                        subgraph ManageTechnical["2. Manage Technical Services Domain"]
                            TS["Technical Service<br>(cmdb_ci_service_technical)"]
                            TSO["Technical Service Offering<br>(service_offering)"]
                            DCG["Dynamic CI Group<br>(cmdb_ci_query_based_service)"]
                            APP["Application Service<br>(cmdb_ci_service_discovered)"]
                            INFRA["Infrastructure CIs<br>(cmdb_ci_server / db / router)"]
                        end

                        BS --> BSO
                        BSO -.-> APP
                        TS --> TSO
                        TSO --> DCG
                        TSO --> APP
                        APP --> INFRA

                        INC_RECORD["Incident Ticket<br>cmdb_ci = Linux Server 01<br>service_offering = SAP Gold Tier"] -.-> INFRA
                        INC_RECORD -.-> BSO
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="tabs-container">
                    <div class="tabs-header">
                        <button class="tab-btn active" data-tab="tab-csdm-fields">ITSM Form CSDM Fields</button>
                        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-csdm-impact">Automated Impact Calculation</button>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel active" id="tab-csdm-fields">
                        <h3>Key CSDM Fields on Task / Incident / Change Forms</h3>
                        <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                            <li><strong>Configuration Item (<code>cmdb_ci</code>):</strong> The specific physical or logical component malfunctioning (e.g., <code>lnx-db-prod-04</code>).</li>
                            <li><strong>Business Service / Service (<code>business_service</code>):</strong> The overarching business capability being delivered (e.g., <em>Global Payroll Processing</em>).</li>
                            <li><strong>Service Offering (<code>service_offering</code>):</strong> The specific tier, region, or SLA variation of the service consumed by the user (e.g., <em>Payroll - North America - Platinum Tier</em>).</li>
                            <li><strong>Dynamic CI Group (<code>cmdb_ci_query_based_service</code>):</strong> A query-driven collection of infrastructure CIs (e.g., <em>All Web Tier Servers in AWS US-East</em>).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-csdm-impact">
                        <h3>Automated Service Impact & Criticality Traversal</h3>
                        <p>
                            When an agent selects a <code>cmdb_ci</code> on an incident, ServiceNow executes relationship 
                            traversal scripts against the <code>cmdb_rel_ci</code> table. The system automatically:
                        </p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Identifies all upstream Application Services and Business Service Offerings relying on that CI.</li>
                            <li>Populates the <strong>Impacted Services</strong> related list (<code>task_cmdb_ci_service</code>).</li>
                            <li>Calculates the maximum <strong>Business Criticality</strong> score to alert the Major Incident team if mission-critical revenue services are at risk.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 5: M2M Intersection Tables -->
            <section class="content-section" id="m2m-intersections">
                <h2>Many-to-Many (M2M) Intersection Tables</h2>
                <p>
                    Enterprise outages rarely affect a single server. ServiceNow uses normalized intersection tables 
                    to associate multiple assets, services, and outages with a single ITSM task:
                </p>

                <table class="data-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Intersection Table</th>
                            <th>Referenced Table A</th>
                            <th>Referenced Table B</th>
                            <th>Operational Purpose</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>task_ci</code></td>
                            <td><code>task</code> (Incident / Change)</td>
                            <td><code>cmdb_ci</code></td>
                            <td>Tracks multiple affected infrastructure assets (e.g., 10 cluster nodes patched during one change).</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>task_cmdb_ci_service</code></td>
                            <td><code>task</code></td>
                            <td><code>cmdb_ci_service</code> / <code>service_offering</code></td>
                            <td>Records all business services impacted by an incident or planned change for stakeholder notifications.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>task_sla</code></td>
                            <td><code>task</code></td>
                            <td><code>contract_sla</code></td>
                            <td>Tracks each running SLA/OLA timer, pause time, and breach condition for the task.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>cmdb_ci_outage</code></td>
                            <td><code>task</code> (Incident / Change)</td>
                            <td><code>cmdb_ci</code> / <code>cmdb_ci_service</code></td>
                            <td>Logs official service downtime, planned maintenance duration, or performance degradation periods.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>sc_item_option_mtom</code></td>
                            <td><code>sc_req_item</code></td>
                            <td><code>sc_item_option</code></td>
                            <td>Stores submitted form variable questions and user answers for a requested catalog item.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

